WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:19.856
John Hector McFarlane turned to us with a gesture of despair and sank into his chair like a

2
00:00:19.916 --> 00:00:20.876
man who is crushed.

3
00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:24.077
"One moment, Lestrade," said Sherlock Holmes.

4
00:00:24.097 --> 00:00:26.138
"Don't take him away just yet.

5
00:00:27.058 --> 00:00:30.619
This gentleman was about to give us his account of this very interesting affair.

6
00:00:31.559 --> 00:00:33.820
His story might aid us in clearing up the mystery."

7
00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:38.689
"I think there will be no difficulty in clearing it up," Inspector Lestrade said grimly.

8
00:00:38.709 --> 00:00:42.030
"Nevertheless, with your permission, Dr.

9
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.591
Watson and I would be very interested in hearing his account," said Holmes.

10
00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:47.051
"Well, Mr.

11
00:00:47.091 --> 00:00:51.533
Holmes, it is difficult for me to refuse you anything because you've helped Scotland Yard in the past."

12
00:00:52.413 --> 00:00:54.553
Lestrade looked at his watch and then at McFarlane.

13
00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:56.434
"I'll give you half an hour."

14
00:00:57.334 --> 00:00:59.455
McFarlane took a deep breath and began his story.

15
00:00:59.475 --> 00:01:02.816
"I must first say that I knew little about Mr.

16
00:01:02.856 --> 00:01:04.648
Jonas Oldacre," said McFarlane.

17
00:01:06.169 --> 00:01:10.431
"His name was familiar to me because my parents were acquainted with him years ago, but they drifted

18
00:01:10.471 --> 00:01:11.051
away from him."

19
00:01:11.992 --> 00:01:17.514
McFarlane was very much surprised, therefore, when Oldacre walked into his office the previous afternoon at about three

20
00:01:17.534 --> 00:01:22.256
o'clock. Oldacre was carrying several sheets of paper covered with scribbled writing.

21
00:01:22.276 --> 00:01:24.937
"Here is my will," said Oldacre.

22
00:01:24.957 --> 00:01:26.658
"I want you, Mr.

23
00:01:26.678 --> 00:01:30.620
McFarlane, to get it into proper legal shape, and I will stay here while you do so."

24
00:01:31.488 --> 00:01:36.470
McFarlane began to copy it and was soon astonished to realize that he was the major beneficiary of

25
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:36.870
the will.

26
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.572
"Oldacre was a strange, ferret-like man," said McFarlane.

27
00:01:40.592 --> 00:01:45.174
"And when I looked up at him, I found his keen gray eyes fixed on me with an

28
00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:46.154
amused expression.

29
00:01:47.094 --> 00:01:48.835
I could hardly believe the terms of the will.

30
00:01:49.695 --> 00:01:52.437
But he explained that he was a bachelor with few living relatives.

31
00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:57.879
He'd known my parents in his youth, and he'd heard that I was a very deserving young man.

32
00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:01.704
I thanked him and continued with my work, which you see here."

33
00:02:02.665 --> 00:02:05.966
At this point McFarlane laid the will and Oldacre's rough draft on our table.

34
00:02:06.886 --> 00:02:08.526
Then McFarlane continued with his story.

35
00:02:08.546 --> 00:02:16.169
"Mr. Oldacre informed me that there were a number of documents—leases, deeds, mortgages—that I should examine," said McFarlane.

36
00:02:16.189 --> 00:02:20.710
"He begged me to come out to his house that night, but insisted that I not mention the

37
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.091
will to my parents until he and I had everything settled.

38
00:02:24.051 --> 00:02:26.552
He said that the will would be a nice surprise for my parents."

39
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:32.644
Oldacre suggested that he and McFarlane dine together at nine o'clock as Oldacre might not be home before

40
00:02:32.684 --> 00:02:38.268
then. So McFarlane sent a telegram to his parents, saying that he had important business to attend to

41
00:02:38.428 --> 00:02:40.409
and that he would be late returning to Blackheath.

42
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.972
He went out to Norwood, but had difficulty finding Oldacre's house.

43
00:02:45.973 --> 00:02:48.595
It was almost half past nine when he knocked on the door.

44
00:02:48.615 --> 00:02:50.196
"One moment!"

45
00:02:50.576 --> 00:02:51.077
said Holmes.

46
00:02:51.097 --> 00:02:52.778
"Who opened the door?"

47
00:02:52.798 --> 00:02:55.620
"A middle-aged woman," replied McFarlane.

48
00:02:56.496 --> 00:02:58.337
"I suppose she was Oldacre's housekeeper."

49
00:02:58.357 --> 00:03:00.717
"Please continue," said Holmes.

50
00:03:01.658 --> 00:03:05.839
McFarlane wiped his damp brow and then told us that the woman had taken him into a sitting

51
00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:07.999
room, where a frugal supper was laid out.

52
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.141
After supper Oldacre showed McFarlane into his bedroom, which contained a heavy safe.

53
00:03:13.161 --> 00:03:17.902
"Mr. Oldacre opened the safe and took out a stack of documents," said McFarlane.

54
00:03:17.922 --> 00:03:21.163
"We went over these together until eleven or twelve o'clock."

55
00:03:22.043 --> 00:03:24.584
When it was time to leave, McFarlane could not find his walking stick.

56
00:03:26.088 --> 00:03:29.551
Oldacre told him not to worry about that because they would be seeing a great deal of each

57
00:03:29.591 --> 00:03:32.073
other from now on, and he could get it the next time they met.

58
00:03:32.093 --> 00:03:37.077
"Mr. Oldacre said we must not disturb the housekeeper, so he showed me out through his own French

59
00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:38.478
window," said McFarlane.

60
00:03:38.498 --> 00:03:43.122
"I left him in his bedroom with the safe open and the papers arranged in packets on the

61
00:03:43.162 --> 00:03:46.485
table. It was so late that I could not get back to Blackheath.

62
00:03:47.345 --> 00:03:51.328
Thus I spent the night at the local hotel and knew nothing of this horrible affair until I

63
00:03:51.389 --> 00:03:52.489
read about it this morning."

64
00:03:52.509 --> 00:03:55.071
"Is there anything more you'd like to ask, Mr.

65
00:03:55.112 --> 00:04:00.806
Holmes?" said Lestrade, who had raised his eyebrows in disbelief several times during this remarkable explanation.

66
00:04:00.826 --> 00:04:04.208
"Not until I have been to Blackheath," said Holmes.

67
00:04:04.228 --> 00:04:07.009
"You mean to Norwood," said Lestrade.

68
00:04:07.029 --> 00:04:11.971
"Oh, yes, that's what I must have meant," said Holmes with a mysterious smile.

69
00:04:11.991 --> 00:04:14.432
"Now come along, Mr.

70
00:04:14.452 --> 00:04:15.892
McFarlane," said Lestrade.

71
00:04:15.912 --> 00:04:18.594
"Two of my policemen are waiting for you in the hall."

72
00:04:19.434 --> 00:04:24.096
The miserable young man stood up and, with a last beseeching glance at us, walked from the room.

73
00:04:25.088 --> 00:04:27.990
The policemen led him away to jail, but Lestrade remained with us.

74
00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.953
Meanwhile Holmes had picked up the rough draft of the will and was looking at the pages with

75
00:04:31.993 --> 00:04:32.753
keen interest.

76
00:04:32.773 --> 00:04:36.936
"There's something strange about that document, wouldn't you say so, Lestrade?"

77
00:04:37.857 --> 00:04:40.279
Holmes pushed the pages across the table to the inspector.

78
00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:43.241
Lestrade looked at them with a puzzled expression.

79
00:04:43.261 --> 00:04:47.964
"I can read the first few lines and some in the middle and one or two at the

80
00:04:48.104 --> 00:04:50.265
end, but the writing in between is very bad."

81
00:04:50.286 --> 00:04:53.588
"It must have been written on a train," said Holmes.

82
00:04:54.524 --> 00:04:59.187
"In fact, an express train, making only one stop between Norwood and London Bridge Station.

83
00:05:00.728 --> 00:05:05.370
The places where you can read the writing represent Norwood, London Bridge, and the stop in between."

84
00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:07.371
Lestrade began to laugh.

85
00:05:07.391 --> 00:05:09.172
"Very good, Mr.

86
00:05:09.232 --> 00:05:11.974
Holmes, but what bearing does this have on the case?"

87
00:05:13.014 --> 00:05:18.977
"It's strange that a man would draw up such an important document in so haphazard a fashion—unless, of

88
00:05:19.057 --> 00:05:20.938
course, he never intended to use it.

89
00:05:21.799 --> 00:05:23.380
This case is not clear to me yet."

90
00:05:24.368 --> 00:05:25.068
"Not clear?"

91
00:05:25.428 --> 00:05:26.129
said Lestrade.

92
00:05:26.149 --> 00:05:27.869
"What could be clearer?

93
00:05:28.689 --> 00:05:32.690
The young man learns he will inherit a fortune, invents a reason to see his client that night,

94
00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:33.991
and kills the older man."

95
00:05:34.011 --> 00:05:38.472
"It strikes me as being just a trifle too obvious," replied Holmes.

96
00:05:38.492 --> 00:05:43.293
"If you were this young man, would you commit your crime on the very night after the will

97
00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:43.893
had been made?

98
00:05:44.754 --> 00:05:47.594
Would you choose a time when the housekeeper had seen you arrive?

99
00:05:49.195 --> 00:05:53.216
Would you take great pains to dispose of the body but leave your own walking stick behind?"

100
00:05:54.276 --> 00:05:55.157
"As for the stick, Mr.

101
00:05:55.197 --> 00:05:59.540
Holmes, you know as well as I do that a criminal is often flustered and does things that

102
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.681
a calm person would not.

103
00:06:01.601 --> 00:06:04.003
McFarlane was very likely afraid to return to the room."

104
00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:05.664
Lestrade paused.

105
00:06:05.684 --> 00:06:08.666
"And there is one more thing that you should note, Mr.

106
00:06:08.706 --> 00:06:13.450
Holmes. As far as we know, none of the important papers were removed from the room.

107
00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:18.653
The prisoner is the one man in the world who had no reason to take them because he

108
00:06:18.693 --> 00:06:20.054
would eventually inherit them."

109
00:06:20.935 --> 00:06:22.336
Holmes seemed struck by this remark.

110
00:06:23.252 --> 00:06:27.437
"I don't mean to deny that some of the evidence is very strongly in your favor," said Holmes.

111
00:06:27.457 --> 00:06:31.181
"I only wish to point out that there may be another theory.

112
00:06:31.982 --> 00:06:33.644
Good morning, Lestrade," he went on.

113
00:06:33.664 --> 00:06:37.348
"I have work to do if I am going to prove this young man's innocence."
